---
/** How the four Today tiles are coloured, and where each line comes from. Rendered by /science/sleep-heart/today-tiles; see scienceTopics.ts. */
import { topicHref } from "../../data/scienceTopics";

/** The four Today tiles, with the rule that colours each one. */
const todayTiles = [
  { label: "Sleep", value: "6h 29", tone: "text-warning", rule: "amber under 7 h" },
  { label: "Resting HR", value: "52", tone: "text-success", rule: "amber +5 \u00b7 red +10" },
  { label: "Recovery", value: "\u219325", tone: "text-success", rule: "red at 12 or under" },
  { label: "Sitting", value: "3 h", tone: "text-text-primary", rule: "amber 10.6 h \u00b7 red 12 h" },
];
---

<div class="mt-[18px] rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:px-[22px]">
  <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 lg:grid-cols-4">
    {todayTiles.map((t) => (
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/8 bg-white/[0.03] p-3">
        <div class="text-[11px] text-text-secondary">{t.label}</div>
        <div class={`mt-1 text-[19px] font-extrabold ${t.tone}`}>{t.value}</div>
        <div class="mt-[7px] font-mono text-[9.5px] text-text-tertiary">{t.rule}</div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="mt-3.5 text-[12.5px] leading-relaxed text-text-tertiary">
    Green means inside the range its evidence calls ordinary. A grey dash
    means Verve has not measured it and will not guess. Sleep, resting rate
    and recovery are explained on their own pages; the sitting line belongs to Movement.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    Each tile takes its colour from the figure it shows. Green means the
    reading is inside the range its evidence calls ordinary, amber that it
    has crossed one line, red that it has crossed a second. A grey dash
    means Verve has not measured it yet and will not guess. The rules,
    one per tile:
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <li>
      <strong class="text-text-primary">Sleep</strong> &mdash; last night's hours
      asleep against the <strong class="text-text-primary">7 to 9 hour</strong>
      range. Amber under seven; ordinary text above nine, because a long
      night is worth noticing but is not a warning. The drift and
      regularity figures inside the tile's page keep their own scales.
      <a href={topicHref("sleep-rhythm")}>Sleep timing &rarr;</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-text-primary">Resting HR</strong> &mdash; the worse of
      two verdicts. Against your own 30-day median: amber around
      <strong class="text-text-primary">5 bpm above</strong>, red around
      <strong class="text-text-primary">10 above</strong>. Against the level
      itself: amber from <strong class="text-text-primary">75</strong>, red
      from <strong class="text-text-primary">80</strong>. Below your usual, and
      under 50, stay green on purpose &mdash; a rested morning and an
      endurance athlete both land there.
      <a href={topicHref("resting-heart-rate")}>Resting heart rate &rarr;</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-text-primary">Recovery</strong> &mdash; how far your heart
      rate fell in the first minute after the last workout. One cited line:
      red at a fall of <strong class="text-text-primary">12 bpm or less</strong>,
      green above it. There is no amber, because no second line has been
      validated; the tile refuses to invent one.
      <a href={topicHref("heart-rate-recovery")}>Heart rate recovery &rarr;</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong class="text-text-primary">Sitting</strong> &mdash; hours today with
      almost no stepping. Amber from <strong class="text-text-primary">10.6
      hours</strong>, red from <strong class="text-text-primary">12</strong>,
      the same two ticks the Movement Balance bar draws.
      <a href={topicHref("movement-balance")}>Movement balance &rarr;</a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    Every number above is read from one thresholds file inside the app,
    the same file the tiles read, so this page and the tiles cannot
    disagree about where a line sits.
  </p>
</div>
